RESULT BECOMES NEW HISTORICAL INTELLIGENCE
Your end-state explicitly requires the system to ingest a large solicitation, extract its requirements/evaluation/pricing/forms, require human verification, research the buyer, analyze historical wins/losses and competitors, support pricing, generate grounded responses, manage submission, capture the result, then manage the resulting contract and renewals.
And your data model isn't merely “documents + chat.” It encompasses pricing, competitors, outcomes, contracts, compliance, experience, public research, and AI run/citation/report history.
Your repo is already substantially beyond a skeleton
You already have real implementation for the major workspaces.
Your pursuit area contains actual routes for pricing, requirements, response, submission, result, intelligence, staffing, documents and contract creation. The component layer already includes competitor briefs, evaluation panels, final-bid handling, four-truth pricing, comparables, a Glide pricing grid, proposal packet gaps, and pursuit intelligence summaries.
Your contract side isn't generic CRUD either: the contract workspace already separates Service Plan, Commercial Terms, Changes and Renewal, which matches the final product model.
Your Python processor already has separate parsers, extractors, evaluation, jobs, PDF quality handling, routing policy and storage logic.
So the goal is not to find another repo and rebuild your app on top of it. The efficient approach is to use several public projects as reference implementations for individual engines.
