The key architectural point
You should not merge 14 codebases into yours.
Your repo should remain the product shell and source of truth:
YOUR REPO
│
├── Pursuit engine
├── Contract engine
├── Intelligence engine
├── Pricing engine
├── Response engine
├── Data Ops / verification
├── Supabase canonical data
└── provenance / four-truth business rules
Then selectively borrow:
RFPilot
    → RFP workflow patterns

AutoRFP
    → question/response interaction patterns

OpenContracts
    → evidence + verification architecture

TenderRadar/OCDS/USAspending
    → public procurement ingestion

CLM references
    → contract workflows

Glide
    → spreadsheet UI

Novel
    → rich response editor

Morphic/Open Deep Research
    → research + source UX

WrenAI
    → structured business analytics
That is the path that prevents you from rebuilding established mechanics without throwing away the genuinely specialized procurement architecture you've already built.
One licensing rule is important: MIT/Apache/CC0 projects are the easiest ones to adapt directly; AGPL repos such as Whereas and Wraft should be treated as design/reference material unless you deliberately accept their copyleft obligations.

